{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 20,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to LastPass for sponsoring PBS Digital Studios"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3400,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our universe started with the Big Bang."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5920,
    "dDurationMs": 3220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But only for the right definition of our universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9620,
    "dDurationMs": 500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10540,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"started\" for that matter. In fact, the Big Bang is probably nothing like what you were taught."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21120,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A hundred years ago, we discovered the beginning of the Universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25080,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Observations of the retreating galaxies by Edwin Hubble and Vesto Slipher,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29720,
    "dDurationMs": 4300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "combined with Einstein's - then - brand-new general theory of relativity,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34020,
    "dDurationMs": 6220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "revealed that our universe is expanding and if we reverse that expansion far enough -"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40240,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mathematically - purely according to Einstein's equations,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44180,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it seems inevitable that all space and mass and energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48820,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "should once have been compacted into an infinitesimally small point -"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53480,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a singularity. It's often said that the universe started with this singularity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57620,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the Big Bang is thought of as the explosive expansion that followed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62080,
    "dDurationMs": 7800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And before the Big Bang singularity, well, they say that there was no 'before' because time and space simply didn't exist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70260,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, if you think you've managed to get your head around this bizarre notion,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74760,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then I have some bad news: that picture is wrong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78620,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And at least according to pretty much every serious physicist who studies the subject."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82940,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The good news is that the truth is way cooler, at least as far as we understand it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88780,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, before a certain crowd starts"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92260,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with \"all the scientists keep changing their minds - they don't know anything\", or \"the Big Bang Theory is just a theory\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99020,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let me be very clear, the evidence for a hot dense early universe is practically incontrovertible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106280,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Cosmic Microwave Background is a direct line of sight to the universe as it was"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111160,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Only a few hundred thousand years after the hypothetical beginning of time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116080,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can see pretty much directly that all space and matter in the universe was once crunched at least a thousand times closer together"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124060,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There's also the relative abundance of simple elements hydrogen and helium in particular"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129069,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Whose ratio is exactly what we expect if the entire universe was a dense"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133880,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "billions of degrees nuclear furnace for the first several minutes of its existence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138600,
    "dDurationMs": 880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139480,
    "dDurationMs": 7480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There's powerful evidence that we should not rewind Einstein's equations that far, at least without introducing some very new physics"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147510,
    "dDurationMs": 7790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For one thing there's also convincing observational evidence that the time before around 10 to the power of negative 32 seconds"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155520,
    "dDurationMs": 4339,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Included a period of extremely rapid expansion called cosmic inflation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160440,
    "dDurationMs": 6319,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We've talked about the reasons we need inflation in previous episodes and I'll come back to it in a bit"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167580,
    "dDurationMs": 6979,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "adding that initial growth spurt solves a couple of the big problems with the Big Bang Theory, but it doesn't change the fact that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175120,
    "dDurationMs": 7100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Rewinding the expansion of the universe even at different speeds still leads us towards the T equals zero"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182720,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "singularity. I'm going to come back to why we need to forget the idea of this singularity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188700,
    "dDurationMs": 6380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Doing so will change the way we think about cosmic inflation and about the beginning of the universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195660,
    "dDurationMs": 6589,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But before we kill the whole idea of the Big Bang singularity, we need to understand what we're killing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202680,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What does it really mean for all of space to be compacted into a single point?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207209,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This idea is especially weird if the universe is infinite"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210959,
    "dDurationMs": 2180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now the universe may or may not be infinite"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213140,
    "dDurationMs": 3029,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but if we can understand this for the infinite case"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216540,
    "dDurationMs": 5419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then getting all of this for the finite case is baby stuff at least by comparison"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222390,
    "dDurationMs": 3020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's tricky to talk about the size of an infinite universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226320,
    "dDurationMs": 7699,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead of the overall volume or radius we talk about the size of an expanding infinite universe in terms of the scale factor"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234810,
    "dDurationMs": 4309,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's the distance between any two points in space at some moment in time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239549,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Relative to their distance at some other reference moments that reference moment is typically taken to be right now"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247940,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the scale factor of the universe is currently one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252740,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Several billion years ago, the scale factor was half, all points in the universe were half as far apart as they are today."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260220,
    "dDurationMs": 3020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So when I talk about rewinding the expansion,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263580,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mean running the clock backwards to track a shrinking scale factor."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268600,
    "dDurationMs": 3100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One way to do that is to keep halving the scale factor."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272060,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Do that enough times and any two points, no matter how far apart they were, will end up"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276500,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as close together as you'd like."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278180,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Do it enough times and the universe could end up as hot and dense as you like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283490,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it'll still be infinite, spatially, the scale factor is incredibly small"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288880,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But an incredibly small number times infinity is still infinity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294260,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Rewinding the universe this way doesn't leave us with a singularity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298280,
    "dDurationMs": 3380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The singularity is when all points are not just next to each other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301980,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but literally in the same spot"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304440,
    "dDurationMs": 2660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at which point temperature and density are infinite."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307400,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That last tiny step is a doozy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310900,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The scale factor goes from incredibly small to zero."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315580,
    "dDurationMs": 2260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the infinite universe becomes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318240,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infinitesimal all points become the same point and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322669,
    "dDurationMs": 2990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "three-dimensional space becomes zero dimensional"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 326300,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's the singularity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328610,
    "dDurationMs": 6559,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We say that it didn't happen in any one place because a point is zero dimensional there weren't"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335389,
    "dDurationMs": 2270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spatial dimensions for it to happen in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338210,
    "dDurationMs": 2329,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the same time we say the Big Bang happened"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341030,
    "dDurationMs": 3739,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Everywhere at once because even the tiniest fraction of a second later"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344900,
    "dDurationMs": 4489,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The universe has infinite size and everywhere is expanding equally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350180,
    "dDurationMs": 4279,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even if the universe is not infinite then whatever space there is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354880,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Comes into being at the same time from that singularity. But what happens to time at the Big Bang singularity?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362780,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To get that you can't think about the universe as having one big clock that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367780,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Rewinds and then winks out of existence of the Big Bang or into existence if you're going forward"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373760,
    "dDurationMs": 3139,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No, you have to think about time in the way Einstein"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377539,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Intended there is no universal clock time is relative"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382520,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Clocks are attached to each observer each moving frame of reference to see what time does at the Big Bang"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388849,
    "dDurationMs": 2690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have to trace a path through space and time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392419,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back to the singularity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 394219,
    "dDurationMs": 7330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We trace a path called a geodesic which in general relativity is the shortest path between two space-time coordinates"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402040,
    "dDurationMs": 3229,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are the grids we use to map space-time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406090,
    "dDurationMs": 7820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Remember that in our rewind all points in the universe get arbitrarily close together before merging at T equals zero"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414430,
    "dDurationMs": 5899,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, that's the same as saying that all geodesics in the universe converge at the Big Bang singularity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 421120,
    "dDurationMs": 3980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the same way all lines of longitude converge at the North Pole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426250,
    "dDurationMs": 1410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so each"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 427660,
    "dDurationMs": 4250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Geodesic tracks earlier and earlier times as it approaches the Big Bang"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432490,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infinite clocks rewinding toward zero and then they all converge and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 438610,
    "dDurationMs": 2990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then what? well, then nothing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442120,
    "dDurationMs": 5869,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All geodesics end at the Big Bang singularity and their timelines end with them"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448270,
    "dDurationMs": 3890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or they start depending on how you want to think about it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452560,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The point is that in the pure Einsteinian picture"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456000,
    "dDurationMs": 7460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There is no before the Big Bang because no time line in this universe can be traced there. This is called"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463460,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "geodesic in completeness and it also happens at the singularity in the center of a black hole all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469750,
    "dDurationMs": 3170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "timelines end this time in the forward direction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472920,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The analogy with the North Pole is a good one and Einstein himself used it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478180,
    "dDurationMs": 6260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Lines of longitude end at the North Pole and it's meaningless to ask what is north of the North Pole?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484960,
    "dDurationMs": 2420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the pure Einsteinian point of view"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 487450,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's meaningless to ask what happened before the Big Bang or after reaching the black hole Center?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494140,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okay, so I'm taking my time to explain something"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496860,
    "dDurationMs": 1930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I already told you is wrong"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498790,
    "dDurationMs": 6739,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it's important because the extreme weirdness of the Big Bang singularity is part of what tells us. It's wrong"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506050,
    "dDurationMs": 6050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Any time you encounter a singularity in the mathematics of a physical theory you have good reason for skepticism"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512860,
    "dDurationMs": 6709,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's probably telling you that your physical theory is incomplete and that you push that theory too far"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520090,
    "dDurationMs": 1890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's what's happening here"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 521980,
    "dDurationMs": 6710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We used general relativity to rewind the universe, but we already know that despite its incredible successes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529300,
    "dDurationMs": 2239,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "GR Is an incomplete theory?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 532220,
    "dDurationMs": 8640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the crazy densities and temperatures of the Big Bang singularity, and just after, GR comes into terrible conflict with quantum mechanics"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540980,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We've talked about that conflict and its possible resolutions before"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544790,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the upshot is that we just don't know how the universe behaves in those conditions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550610,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we do know that pure general relativity is not a good description"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556120,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so he probably shouldn't believe its prediction that all space was compacted into a single point and that this is where"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563680,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Time started. Ok. So what are the alternatives?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 567800,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Can we really track Geodesics?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570500,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the timelines they embody through the Big Bang and out the other side"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575270,
    "dDurationMs": 2419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If so, what do we find there?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578200,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are several possibilities and they deserve their own episodes and we'll actually get to those soon"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 584440,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But to whet your appetite, first up, cosmic inflation can offer a temporary reprieve from the singularity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592220,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eternal inflation suggests that our universe appeared as a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596540,
    "dDurationMs": 2390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "regularly expanding bubble in an"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599540,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unimaginably larger continuously inflating space-time in that case before the Big Bang was a period of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607130,
    "dDurationMs": 2270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exponential expansion that could have lasted"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609920,
    "dDurationMs": 1080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "indefinitely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611000,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We'll get to the nitty-gritty of that with its inflow tongs and bubble universes real soon"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 616580,
    "dDurationMs": 3019,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are also various cyclic universe options"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619760,
    "dDurationMs": 3979,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the first cyclic universe idea was the Big Bounce in which the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623900,
    "dDurationMs": 6619,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gravitational attraction of all matter in the universe was enough to cause it to re-collapse and then presumably bounce outwards again"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631190,
    "dDurationMs": 3559,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We now know that there isn't anywhere near enough matter to do that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635540,
    "dDurationMs": 2780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unless we bring in string theory the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639290,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Steinhardt-Turok model suggests that our universe floats in a higher dimensional space"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644780,
    "dDurationMs": 2839,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "living on geometric objects called brains"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648440,
    "dDurationMs": 4369,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collisions between those brains initiate cycles of expansion of contraction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653360,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then there's Roger Penrose"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655520,
    "dDurationMs": 5599,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Conformal cyclic cosmology it's even weirder because it postulates the infinite future"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661550,
    "dDurationMs": 2179,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "boundary of an eternally expanding universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664819,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Looks like the Big Bang of a new universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668489,
    "dDurationMs": 3739,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mathematically so our heat death is someone else's Big Bang?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672689,
    "dDurationMs": 4099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are some less abstract ways to get a new universe out of an old one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677009,
    "dDurationMs": 6650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for example an extreme quantum fluctuation could initiate a new Big Bang given infinite time or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684269,
    "dDurationMs": 5059,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The same amount of time could lead to all particles randomly converging back to the same spot"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690020,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or maybe black holes birth new universes as in Lee Smolin's \"Fecund Universe\" hypothesis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696420,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There's a poetry to that last one. The geodesics approaching the black hole singularity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701640,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Become the geodesics emerging from the new Big Bang singularity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706319,
    "dDurationMs": 2719,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "people love cyclic and regenerating universes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709079,
    "dDurationMs": 6109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They appeal to our sense of narrative which might be a reason to be wary of these hypotheses"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715919,
    "dDurationMs": 3710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now they also appeal to our intuition for causality"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720569,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Things happen because prior events caused them many of our ideas"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725789,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just push back the uncomfortable something from nothing moments"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730589,
    "dDurationMs": 6109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physicists have a thing or two to say about that from quantum fluctuations from nothing - Stephen Hawking's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736949,
    "dDurationMs": 4669,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "timeless interpretation of internal inflation that draws on the holographic principle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 742600,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all things we'll discuss in the future as we travel beyond the beginning of Space-Time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 750200,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A big thank you to LastPass for sponsoring previous digital studios"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753640,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "LastPass remembers your passwords for you by Auto filling your usernames and passwords"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 759400,
    "dDurationMs": 8260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "LastPass is designed to store the count walkouts, which means you won't need to answer security questions like 'What is your favorite childhood pet's name?'"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767660,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or, 'what street did your paternal grandmother live on growing up?' they protect your data and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772960,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "give you the power to make your passwords impenetrable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775860,
    "dDurationMs": 6140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You could also easily and safely share passwords through LastPass if others need to access your accounts"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782350,
    "dDurationMs": 4219,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the service works on mobile sites and on apps for iOS and Android"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787090,
    "dDurationMs": 7969,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The service provides unlimited password storage. There are additional service options available. Click on the link in description below to start today"
    } ]
  } ]
}
